==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|dead-ringer|deadringer}}

===Etymology===
See {{m|en|ringer||substitute}} and {{m|en|ring the changes}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-dead ringer.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} Someone or something that very closely resembles another; someone or something easily mistaken for another.
#: {{syn|en|spitting image}}
#: {{ux|en|He is a '''dead ringer''' for his grandfather at that age.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Edward Wyatt|title=Bret Easton Ellis: The Man in the Mirror|work=The New York Times|date=2005-08-07|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2005/08/07/arts/bret-easton-ellis-the-man-in-the-mirror.html|issn=0362-4331|passage= In 1987, in “The Rules of Attraction,” he wrote about casual sex and obsessive drug use among bored students of a '''dead ringer''' for Bennington College, which Mr. Ellis attended.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2018-02-28|author=Justine Jordan|title=Asymmetry by Lisa Halliday review – a dizzying debut|work=The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2018/feb/28/asymmetry-lisa-halliday-review|passage=Twentysomething Alice works in publishing, so is instantly in awe of this old man offering her chocolate with a trembling hand. He is world-famous writer Ezra Blazer, a '''dead ringer''' for Philip Roth, with whom Halliday had a relationship in her 20s.}}

====Usage notes====
Used with ''for'' to convey whom or what is being resembled.

